<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Washington, D.C.</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as District of Columbia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Washington, D.C., is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potomac-river">Potomac River</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Planned on a site chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, it has been the seat of the federal government since 1800 and houses Congress, the White House and the Supreme Court. Its name is often used for the United States government itself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/washington-dc.jpg" alt="Washington, D.C." width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralf Roletschek - Infos über Fahrräder auf fahrradmonteur.de Wikis in der Ausbildung · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12-07-13-washington-by-RalfR-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Opposition to nuclear weapons became national policy under David Lange: in 1985 his government refused a visit by the American destroyer USS Buchanan because the United States would neither confirm nor deny whether it carried nuclear weapons, and in 1986 <b>Washington</b> suspended its ANZUS obligations to New Zealand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the family’s Garden Palace and City Palace in Vienna; sales from it after the Second World War, when the family lost its estates in Czechoslovakia, helped sustain the dynasty, and Leonardo da Vinci’s Ginevra de’ Benci was sold to the National Gallery of Art in <b>Washington</b> in 1967.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His independent foreign policy and rumoured canal negotiations with other powers alarmed <b>Washington</b>, which helped to force him out in 1909.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1903 treaty granting the United States the Canal Zone was signed for Panama by a Frenchman, Philippe Bunau-Varilla, before the Panamanian delegation had reached <b>Washington</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>It has spread to the Dominican Republic, Puerto Rico, France and Canada, and major temples stand in Miami, Oakland, Boston, New York City and <b>Washington, D.C.</b></blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The fasces, the Roman bundle of rods tied round an axe that gave fascism its name, was used before 1914 mostly by left-wing, liberal and republican movements; it appears on the Sheldonian Theater at Oxford and on the Lincoln Memorial in <b>Washington, D.C.</b></blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
